  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nCentral idea: We will use a multi-resolution optimization strategy combined with coordinate-wise updates and state-dependent mutation to explore the function space more effectively. Instead of relying solely on local refinement, we'll synthesize powerful prior knowledge from known optimal constructions (like the parent solution) and use curriculum learning to gradually increase complexity. This helps escape local minima by combining fast, low-complexity updates with purposeful, knowledge-guided variations.\n\nThe primary bottleneck in the parent approach is its over-reliance on gradient-based local refinement. Our method injects coordinate-wise perturbations with amplitude proportional to the current solution's confidence level, combined with amplified triggers from the periodic structure of optimal solutions.\n\nRationale: This reduces gradient-based noise during local refinement while ensuring meaningful global exploration. Dynamic curriculum periods ensure uniform progress across representational scales, and local modifications are informed by the parent solution's structure to maintain feasibility.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentsolution: The function h is discretized as a vector of length n_points with constraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] * dx = 1, where dx = 2.0 / n_points\n\nObjective: Minimize C5(f) = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx, computed as:\n- C5_val = max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')) * dx\n\nRepresentation:\n- Use block_size = total_blocks parameterization to manage complexity\n- Embed parent solution as scaled Chebyshev polynomials\n\nVariable management:\n- Maintain active set of atoms for mutation\n- Use dynamic target(period_initial=50) to determine curriculum progression\n\nAlgorithm components:\n- Hierarchical curriculum attribute determines memory in states\n- Three-phase training curriculum:\n    1. Vector to goal scale (low-frequency)\n    2. Compile hypersolver training\n    3. Full optimization phase (high-frequency)\n- Progressive curriculum parameter adjustments:\n    \u2207_adapt = \u03b1 * \u2207_local + (1 - \u03b1) * \u2207_curriculum\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStage 0: Initialization\nInput: seed, budget_s, **kwargs\nOutput: h_rep, c5_val, n_points=560\n\nSteps:\n1. Set parent solution baseline and modes\n2. Parameter deployment:\n    curriculum_start = 50\n    curriculum_end = 700\n    total_blocks = 14 (fixed)\n    period_initial = 50  # curriculum steps\n\n3. Hierarchy-initialize blocks with parent solution structure:\n    - Interpolate parent solution onto new hierarchy\n    - Apply value morphing factors (\u03c7_i)\n\n4. Progressive initialization:\n    - Time.allocate_budget(budget_s)\n    - Initialize \u03b8 from parent's harmonic structure\n    - Set curriculum_phase = 'vector_to_goal'\n\n5. Full targeting loop:\n    For training_cycle in [1, 2, ..., curriculum_end]:\n        if time_elapsed > budget_s: break\n        if training_cycle < curriculum_start:\n            Apply smooth\u2207 = 0.8 * smooth_grad(h_prev)\n        else:\n            dynamic_top = calculate_adaptive_freq(training_cycle)\n            coarse_targeted = morph_target(parent_morphs[training_cycle])\n            h_rep += evolve_coarse_block(coarse_targeted)\n\n        c5_val = C5(h_rep)\n        if c5_val > best_found: apply curriculum strategy\n        else: revert with curvature guidance\n\n6. Final repair:\n    - Apply block-policy repair with state retention\n    - Match integral constraint: rescale_horizon[training_cycle]\n\n7. Validation:\n    - Re-evaluate C5 with reference implementation\n    - Ensure finite value and data integrity\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\nDefault configuration:\n- curriculum_periods = [50, 150, 400]\n- target_lengths = [8, 24, 72, 200, 500]\n- smooth_factor = 0.45 (cyclic adjustment)\n- curriculum_decay = 0.85\n\nHypersolver settings:\n- Population = 30 parents\n- Reversion_level = 0.95\n\nPerturbation controls:\n- mutation_ratio = 0.5 (adjust by lag correlation)\n- variable_surface = 'sawtooth' (fixed while {training_cycle} < 400)\n\nPeriodic triggers:\n- Period embedding: embed_period(parent_solution, 0.3)\n- Energy factor: 50 * math.exp(-cycles/150)\n\nProgressive switching:\nTrigger at cycle 50: move to coarse refinement\nTrigger at cycle 150: activate block mutation\nTrigger at cycle 300: apply parent_morph integration\n\nStop conditions: \n- max_curriculum without improvement\n- budget depletion\n- stagnation index >= 0.95\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\nEstimate:\n- Preprocessing: O(n_points log n_points)\n- Curriculum: O(curriculum_end)\n- Repair phase: O(parent_solution_length * N_train)\n\nTime profile:\n- Training cycle allocation: priority to \n    \u2192 curriculum_target\n    \u2192 threshold sp\u00e9cifique\n    \u2192 l'hypersolver\n\nBudget allocation:\n- Phase 1: 200 s\n- Phase 2: 700/local_games\n- Phase 3: budget_s - phase1\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n- Check feasible constraints:\n    \u2192 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n    \u2192 \u03a3h * dx = 1 (tolerance 1e-8)\n    \u2192 C5_val finite and valid\n- Re-evaluate with reference implementation\n- Restore parent functionality if timeout\n- Return format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n- Fallback mechanism: restore last_valid if repair fails\n\nSafety checks:\n- Never violate [0,1] or integral constraints\n- Periodic restarts at unknown states\n- h repair checks by gradientprojection",
